'''accumulate''' (ạ-kū′mụ̄-lāt), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''accumulated'', ppr. ''accumulating''. [< L. ''accumulatus'', pp. of ''accumulare'', heap up, < ''ad'', to, + ''cumulare'', heap, < ''cumulus'', a heap: see ''cumulate'' and ''cumulus''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To heap up; collect or bring together; make a pile, mass, or aggregation of: as, to ''accumulate'' earth or stones; to ''accumulate'' money or sorrows.
{{smaller block|<poem>Never pray more; abandon all remorse;
On horror’s head horrors ''accumulate''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' To form by heaping up or collecting the parts or elements of; obtain by gathering in; amass: as, to ''accumulate'' wealth. [Rare in the physical sense, as in the first extract.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Soon the young captive prince shall roll in fire,
And all his race ''accumulate'' the pyre.</poem>
{{right|''J. Barlow'', Columbiad, iii. 362. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

